{"step_index":108,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:01Z","thinking":"**Selecting a theorem to prove**\n\nConsidering which theorem to attempt first. The objective is to replace \"sorry\" in either `prove_e20263a9` or `refute_e20263a9`. Decided to start with `prove_e20263a9` and attempt a proof.\n```lean\ntheorem prove_e20263a9 : ∃ (x : ℤ), x > 5 :=\nbegin\n  use 6,\n  exact by linarith,\nend\n\ntheorem refute_e20263a9 : ∀ (x : ℤ), x ≤ 5 :=\nsorry\n```\n\n\n**Analyzing the Challenge Formulation**\n\nExamining the `Candidate` proposition to understand its components. Noticed the use of `Type` instead of `Type*` for the vertex set `V` and associated constraints. This constrains the potential counterexamples. The goal is to figure out what `Candidate` is asserting, and then to either prove it or disprove it by finding a counterexample.\n\n\n**Evaluating Roman Domination Number**\n\nThe `romanDominationNumber` is the minimum weight among all Roman dominating functions. Determined the set of Roman dominating functions is non-empty, as the constant function `fun _ => 2` is a valid example. This implies the minimum is well-defined.\n\n\n**Formulating the Proof Strategy**\n\nConsidering how to prove the inequality relating the graph's radius and its Roman domination number.  The core idea is to show, for any Roman dominating function, the Roman weight is at least the radius plus one. This approach involves relating the definitions of radius and Roman domination. Attempting to connect the radius of the graph to the properties of Roman dominating functions.\n\n**Connecting Radius to Domination**\n\nThe radius is defined as the minimum eccentricity, where eccentricity is the minimum distance such that the ball around a vertex covers the whole graph.  If the radius is `r`, there is a vertex `v` such that the ball of radius `r` centered at `v` covers the whole graph. This suggests how to construct a Roman dominating function.\n\n**Constructing a Roman Function**\n\nIntending to construct a Roman dominating function that uses information about `v`.  Set the function to `f v = 2`, and for all nodes outside the ball of radius `r-1` around `v`, set their value to be 0 or 1. It is unclear yet, whether this would work. If radius is r, then we can have a node at least distance `r` away from `v`. If the node is `u`, the ball of radius r at `v` includes `u`. The ball of radius `r-1` does not. We need a way to relate this.\n\n**Examining Dominating Properties**\n\nNow examining what `IsRomanDominatingFunction` and its implications mean. If `f` is a Roman dominating function, every vertex is \"covered\" either by a value of 2 at that vertex, or by a value of 1 on an adjacent vertex. Considering how the ball of radius `r` at `v` relates to the Roman domination. The radius `r` implies that every node is at most distance `r` away from `v`.\n\n\n**Evaluating the Conjecture's Validity**\n\nConsidering whether the proposed inequality holds for *every* Roman dominating function. Recognizing that if the inequality holds for the *minimum* Roman domination number, it holds for all functions. Thus, focusing on proving `radius(G) + 1 <= gamma_R(G)`.\n\n**Reflecting on Potential Counterexamples**\n\nQuestioning whether the conjecture could be refuted and recalling previous tests. Realizing tests were limited to specific graph families for orders 8, 9, and 10, not exhaustive searches. Suspecting potential counterexamples may exist within the untested graphs of these orders.\n\n**Re-examining Previous Proof Strategies**\n\nRecalling an assumption made in a previous proof sketch. Highlighting an assumed bound on the radius of an auxiliary graph. Questioning the validity of this auxiliary graph and its assumptions when constructing the proof.\n\n**Addressing edge case conditions**\n\nExamining the implications of $S$ being empty where $f(v)=1$ for all v.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\\nimport z3\\n\\ndef search_counterexample(n, target_r, target_rd):\\n    # Search for an undirected graph G on n vertices with:\\n    # 1. Connected (HasRadius)\\n    # 2. Radius >= target_r\\n    # 3. Exists a Roman dominating function of weight <= target_rd\\n    # If target_r + 1 > target_rd, this is a COUNTEREXAMPLE!\\n    s = z3.Solver()\\n    \\n    # Adjacency matrix: adj[i][j]\\n    adj = [[z3.Bool(f'e_{i}_{j}') for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        s.add(z3.Not(adj[i][i]))\\n        for j in range(n):\\n            s.add(adj[i][j] == adj[j][i])\\n            \\n    # Distance matrix: dist[i][j] in {0..n}\\n    dist = [[z3.Int(f'd_{i}_{j}') for j in range(n)] for i in range(n)]\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in range(n):\\n            s.add(dist[i][j] >= 0, dist[i][j] <= n)\\n            if i == j:\\n                s.add(dist[i][j] == 0)\\n            else:\\n                s.add(dist[i][j] >= 1)\\n                # If adjacent, dist is 1\\n                s.add(z3.Implies(adj[i][j], dist[i][j] == 1))\\n                # Triangle inequality / shortest path constraints\\n                for k in range(n):\\n                    s.add(dist[i][j] <= dist[i][k] + dist[k][j])\\n                    \\n    # For every pair (i, j) with dist[i][j] > 1, there must be a neighbor k of i with dist[k][j] = dist[i][j] - 1\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in range(n):\\n            if i != j:\\n                # either dist == 1 and adj[i][j], or exists k\\n                s.add(z3.Implies(dist[i][j] == 1, adj[i][j]))\\n                conds = [z3.And(adj[i][k], dist[k][j] == dist[i][j] - 1) for k in range(n) if k != i]\\n                s.add(z3.Or(dist[i][j] == 1, z3.Or(conds)))\\n                \\n    # HasRadius: every pair has finite distance <= n-1\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in range(n):\\n            s.add(dist[i][j] <= n - 1)\\n            \\n    # Radius >= target_r: for EVERY vertex v, max_u dist[v][u] >= target_r\\n    \n<truncated 1379 bytes>","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"15000","toolAction":"\"Running command\"","toolSummary":"\"Run Z3 search for counterexample\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
